; onehotc_col_at_grid_extent: a column at the grid extent must raise.
;
; The mirror of onehotr_row_at_grid_extent. The grid is 4x1, so the only
; valid column is 0, and column 2 addresses no line of the grid.
;
; A column cannot wrap the way ONEHOTR's row can -- nothing multiplies by
; it -- but it does not need to. The address is `ri * cols + col`, so an
; out-of-extent column still lands outside the column it named: on a 2x2
; grid, column 3 addressed flat indices 3 and 5. Index 3 is a declared
; variable in the wrong column; index 5 is past the four the model
; declared. The Rust VM wrote both -- coefficients accumulate into a
; sparse map that grows to fit whatever index it is handed -- so it
; created `linear[5]` and `quad(3, 5)` out of nothing and halted
; successfully, while xqvm_py raised. A model that solves cleanly and
; answers wrongly.
;
; The grid is deliberately not square, and the column is deliberately
; inside the row count, so this pins the extent that is actually
; addressed rather than merely rejecting a large number: a check against
; the row count would accept column 2 here, and against a square grid the
; two are indistinguishable.

PUSH 4
BQMX r0

PUSH 4           ; rows
PUSH 1           ; cols
RESIZE r0

PUSH 2           ; col: outside [0, cols) but inside [0, rows)
PUSH 1           ; penalty
ONEHOTC r0
HALT
